<p data-block-id="e4b7e1b3-ec89-4e32-8fa5-303eb9ca4e8e">Cleaner freight projects also support Canada’s broader plan to cut emissions from medium- and heavy-duty vehicles. The C$97.3 million funding supports Canada’s long-term move to a lower-carbon transportation system. It combines infrastructure investment, fleet upgrades, and education programs.</p>
<h2 data-block-id="74c572d3-93f7-4340-b85a-a097f729b17c"><strong>Closing the Emissions Gap in Transport</strong></h2>
<p data-block-id="9098b174-4df9-452f-8d27-31ee4bef8dc3">Transportation emissions remain high in Canada. Power plant emissions have fallen in recent years, but transport emissions have been slower to drop.</p>
<figure id="attachment_41465" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-41465" style="width: 650px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img decoding="async" class="wp-image-41465" src="https://carboncredits.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Canada-Transport-Sector-GHG-Emissions-1990-2023-300x188.jpg" alt="Canada Transport Sector GHG Emissions (1990-2023)" width="650" height="408" srcset="https://carboncredits.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Canada-Transport-Sector-GHG-Emissions-1990-2023-300x188.jpg 300w, https://carboncredits.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Canada-Transport-Sector-GHG-Emissions-1990-2023-1024x643.jpg 1024w, https://carboncredits.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Canada-Transport-Sector-GHG-Emissions-1990-2023-768x483.jpg 768w, https://carboncredits.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Canada-Transport-Sector-GHG-Emissions-1990-2023-696x437.jpg 696w, https://carboncredits.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Canada-Transport-Sector-GHG-Emissions-1990-2023-1068x671.jpg 1068w, https://carboncredits.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Canada-Transport-Sector-GHG-Emissions-1990-2023.jpg 1181w" sizes="(max-width: 650px) 100vw, 650px" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-41465" class="wp-caption-text">Data Source: Environment and Climate Change Canada (ECCC)</figcaption></figure>
<p data-block-id="3d0413e9-084b-4b6d-8383-611f1a5e3d8a"><a href="https://carboncredits.com/canadas-stellantis-dispute-what-it-means-for-evs-and-net-zero-goals/">Electric vehicles</a> produce zero tailpipe emissions. Canada’s electricity grid is about 83% non-emitting. So, when powered by it, EVs can greatly reduce carbon output. Heavy-duty vehicle upgrades and freight efficiency improvements also provide measurable reductions.</p>
<p data-block-id="6ed80910-f9ee-4e53-8292-2480c353c37a">The new C$97.3 million funding helps close infrastructure gaps and prepares communities for increased EV adoption. It also sends a signal to private investors. Public funding often helps unlock additional private capital in <a href="https://carboncredits.com/clean-energy-investment-hits-record-2-3t-in-2025-evs-and-renewables-lead-the-surge/">clean energy</a> and infrastructure projects.</p>
<p data-block-id="8fdfccb9-5ff0-409d-b00f-7a54d0ba7c08">Moreover, the installation of 8,000 new chargers will increase national charging coverage. Freight modernization projects will reduce diesel use, while education programs will improve awareness and workforce skills.</p>
<p data-block-id="00220ea7-f372-488e-bc99-7e0a5e88a111">These steps support Canada’s commitment to reducing emissions by 40–45% below 2005 levels by 2030, while moving toward net-zero by 2050.</p>
<p data-block-id="62dcc49b-5815-4c94-8758-01979430377b">The C$97.3 million investment is one part of a broader national effort. As charging networks grow and fleets modernize, Canada’s transportation sector may gradually lower its carbon footprint. Further policy support, infrastructure development, and private investment will determine the pace of that transition.</p>
